WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:08.129
OK, I'll give it to you straight --

00:00:08.129 --> 00:00:14.177
we need at least four trillion
dollars per year by 2030

00:00:14.177 --> 00:00:15.929
to avert a climate crisis.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.891
And right now, that money
simply isn't flowing

00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:23.144
at the rate we need
to keep this planet habitable.

00:00:24.563 --> 00:00:26.898
Now I work with a lot
of banks and investors,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.984
and everyone who I talk to --

00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:31.861
and I work with some
of the leading ones around the world --

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:33.738
they get what needs to be done.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.116
They're aware of the climate risks

00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:40.120
and they're actually really excited
about the unprecedented opportunities

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.664
to finance the green economy
of the future.

00:00:43.123 --> 00:00:44.708
And here's the good news.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:49.004
Over 450 financial institutions
around the world

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:52.257
have committed to aligning
their financing to net-zero

00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:53.425
by 2050.

00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:55.260
And collectively,

00:00:55.302 --> 00:01:00.056
these financial institutions
manage over 130 trillion dollars.

00:01:00.807 --> 00:01:05.312
OK, so we need trillions,
we've got trillions.

00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:06.813
Problem solved, right?

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:08.231
Shortest TED Talk ever.

00:01:08.231 --> 00:01:09.482
(Laughter)

00:01:09.524 --> 00:01:11.276
I wish that were the case.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.196
But now, as we're working
to deliver on those commitments,

00:01:15.196 --> 00:01:17.490
it's actually proving to be really hard.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.661
Like, really hard to deploy real money
into real technologies

00:01:21.661 --> 00:01:24.039
that will actually
really decarbonize the world.

00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:28.585
And as a result, too much of that money,

00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:32.213
it's sitting on the sidelines,
waiting to be put to work.

00:01:33.798 --> 00:01:36.092
But I think we can change that.

00:01:36.092 --> 00:01:37.761
And it's not even what I think --

00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:39.012
I know we can,

00:01:39.054 --> 00:01:41.931
because it's based on what banks
and investors tell me

00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:44.726
about what they need
to go fast and go far.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.688
And what they need is help
from people like you and me.

00:01:50.106 --> 00:01:51.566
So to bring this idea to life,

00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:56.363
let's zoom in on one part of the economy,
one that we can all relate to --

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:57.656
it's close to home.

00:01:57.697 --> 00:01:59.366
Let's talk about housing.

00:01:59.658 --> 00:02:04.079
Because, believe it or not,
the challenges we're facing with housing

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:06.164
and the challenges
we're facing with climate

00:02:06.206 --> 00:02:07.374
are inextricably linked,

00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:09.876
so as we solve for one,
we can solve for the other.

00:02:11.211 --> 00:02:14.673
You see, we're facing two concurrent
challenges right now, with housing.

00:02:14.673 --> 00:02:17.384
On one hand, there's
an affordability challenge.

00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:20.887
There's just a scarcity of homes
that are affordable right now.

00:02:20.887 --> 00:02:22.681
Here in the United States,

00:02:22.681 --> 00:02:25.517
there's a scarcity
of over seven million homes

00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:29.354
that people who live at or below
the poverty line can afford to rent.

00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:31.981
And the other affordability challenge

00:02:31.981 --> 00:02:35.193
is it's less affordable
than ever before to live in one,

00:02:35.235 --> 00:02:37.445
because they're so energy-inefficient.

00:02:38.238 --> 00:02:41.741
These older, less energy-efficient homes

00:02:41.741 --> 00:02:44.744
tend to be in Black
and <b>B</b>rown neighborhoods

00:02:44.744 --> 00:02:46.663
due to the structural nature of poverty.

00:02:47.163 --> 00:02:48.331
And get this --

00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:52.460
a recent study found
that the average renter who is Black

00:02:52.502 --> 00:02:56.589
pays over 250 dollars more
per year in utility bills

00:02:56.631 --> 00:02:58.299
than one who is white.

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:03.221
OK, so we need to build
a lot more affordable housing.

00:03:03.263 --> 00:03:04.556
Great.

00:03:04.597 --> 00:03:07.517
But that's when we run
into the climate challenge,

00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:09.352
because the buildings that we live in

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:12.230
are one of the leading contributors
to the greenhouse gasses

00:03:12.272 --> 00:03:13.940
that are fueling climate change.

00:03:13.982 --> 00:03:15.984
And if we take
a business-as-usual approach

00:03:16.025 --> 00:03:17.652
to just building all this housing,

00:03:17.694 --> 00:03:20.196
we'll only exacerbate
the climate challenge.

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:21.448
Think about the emissions

00:03:21.448 --> 00:03:23.491
from all that cement
and steel and concrete

00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:25.785
that goes into a high-rise, for example.

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:28.329
So, a pretty sticky challenge.

00:03:29.122 --> 00:03:32.876
But that's why I love working
in sustainable finance and investing,

00:03:32.917 --> 00:03:35.128
because we get to take
challenges like this

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.505
and transform them to opportunities,

00:03:37.547 --> 00:03:40.049
specifically financing opportunities.

00:03:40.049 --> 00:03:44.012
Opportunities to deploy debt, equity,
grants by governments,

00:03:44.012 --> 00:03:45.346
investors and banks.

00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:49.058
And in the housing sector,
the financing opportunities are enormous.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.435
We recently did a study.

00:03:50.435 --> 00:03:54.481
We found over a trillion dollars' worth
of things that banks can finance

00:03:54.522 --> 00:03:57.233
to lower the emissions
associated with our housing.

00:03:58.067 --> 00:03:59.694
OK, great, so what will it take?

00:03:59.694 --> 00:04:02.155
How can we actually get this done?

00:04:02.155 --> 00:04:03.823
Well, here's the catch.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.827
You see, banks work
within prescribed risk thresholds,

00:04:07.827 --> 00:04:09.746
and some of the things we need most --

00:04:09.746 --> 00:04:13.792
like replacements for that high-carbon
cement, steel, concrete --

00:04:13.792 --> 00:04:16.044
they're being developed
by companies right now

00:04:16.085 --> 00:04:17.921
that are still relatively early-stage,

00:04:17.921 --> 00:04:20.507
and might not yet fit
within those risk thresholds.

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:25.220
Similarly, building affordable homes
in a low-income community

00:04:25.261 --> 00:04:26.805
or a low-income country

00:04:26.846 --> 00:04:31.893
might not reach the return thresholds
that investors are looking for.

00:04:32.936 --> 00:04:34.813
When I talk to banks and investors,

00:04:34.854 --> 00:04:38.149
they say they need
the right type of public policies,

00:04:38.149 --> 00:04:41.277
they need the right type of capital
and they need demand

00:04:41.319 --> 00:04:43.071
in order to put this money to work.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.406
So that's where you and I can come in,

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:48.368
because there are some simple moves
that you and I can do

00:04:48.368 --> 00:04:52.497
that can help to create the conditions
that will allow this money to flow.

00:04:52.497 --> 00:04:55.333
I'm going to talk you
through these different moves,

00:04:55.375 --> 00:04:57.669
and as I'm talking, just bear in mind,

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:00.046
these moves are not exclusive to housing.

00:05:00.046 --> 00:05:04.926
We could apply this playbook in any sector
where we need faster climate action.

00:05:05.343 --> 00:05:06.761
OK, let's dive in.

00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:12.058
The first thing that we can do
to really move these trillions of dollars

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.645
is make the climate-housing connection
matter to government.

00:05:16.354 --> 00:05:19.649
Governments have a huge role to play
in climate finance.

00:05:19.691 --> 00:05:21.568
But when I talk to banks and investors,

00:05:21.609 --> 00:05:24.487
there's one particular form
of government funding

00:05:24.487 --> 00:05:27.031
that they say would make
a huge difference,

00:05:27.031 --> 00:05:29.951
and it's called blended
or catalytic finance.

00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:33.872
You see, the private sector,
banks and investors,

00:05:33.913 --> 00:05:35.540
they have a lot of capital,

00:05:35.582 --> 00:05:38.626
but not necessarily
always the right risk appetite.

00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:39.919
On the other hand,

00:05:39.961 --> 00:05:42.171
governments are willing
to take some risks,

00:05:42.213 --> 00:05:45.466
especially for things
like the climate or shelter equity,

00:05:45.508 --> 00:05:47.677
but they don't have as much capital.

00:05:47.719 --> 00:05:52.390
So what blended finance involves
is governments deploying their finance

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.267
in instruments like guarantees,

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.811
or more concessional forms of lending,

00:05:56.853 --> 00:05:58.938
and this allows
that private-sector finance

00:05:58.938 --> 00:06:02.108
to flow when the governments
take on more of that risk.

00:06:02.108 --> 00:06:04.736
And this kind of blended
or catalytic finance

00:06:04.736 --> 00:06:08.364
has proven to be tremendously effective
in areas like affordable housing,

00:06:08.406 --> 00:06:11.451
where a little bit of government money
can go a long way.

00:06:12.201 --> 00:06:15.288
OK, great, so that's how we can
get some money flowing,

00:06:15.288 --> 00:06:17.582
but there is more that we need
from governments,

00:06:17.624 --> 00:06:19.292
and I call them the three p's:

00:06:19.292 --> 00:06:22.545
public policies, programs, permits.

00:06:22.962 --> 00:06:24.213
And to bring this to life,

00:06:24.255 --> 00:06:27.008
let me tell you a story
of a bank that I'm working with.

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:28.760
They have bold ambitions.

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:33.723
They want to bring all the financing
in line with net-zero targets by 2050,

00:06:33.723 --> 00:06:35.642
including their financing for homes.

00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:39.896
And as we worked
to bring this ambition to life,

00:06:39.938 --> 00:06:42.106
we ran into three key challenges.

00:06:42.982 --> 00:06:45.026
In a lot of the markets
where they operate,

00:06:45.068 --> 00:06:48.321
the right public policies
just weren't in place.

00:06:48.321 --> 00:06:51.741
Minimum energy-efficiency standards
just didn't exist.

00:06:52.992 --> 00:06:56.704
Moreover, the programs
to incentivize homeowners

00:06:56.704 --> 00:06:59.499
to put solar panels on their roof
or retrofit their homes,

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.168
the kind of thing that the bank
was willing to finance,

00:07:02.168 --> 00:07:04.420
they just weren't available
at sufficient scale.

00:07:04.462 --> 00:07:07.966
And finally, when the bank was looking
for big-ticket things to finance,

00:07:07.966 --> 00:07:13.096
like entirely new housing developments
that were affordable and low-emissions,

00:07:13.137 --> 00:07:15.765
they found that projects
were held up in permitting.

00:07:16.182 --> 00:07:18.935
And so their good intentions
remained stalled,

00:07:18.977 --> 00:07:20.645
and so did the money.

00:07:21.312 --> 00:07:23.773
But that's where people
like you and I can come in,

00:07:23.773 --> 00:07:25.650
we the voters and constituents,

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.695
because we can demand
that governments do this.

00:07:28.695 --> 00:07:31.155
We can demand that they
put in place that financing,

00:07:31.197 --> 00:07:33.032
those programs, those permits.

00:07:33.074 --> 00:07:34.242
And look, I get it.

00:07:34.283 --> 00:07:38.538
Right now, it's a little bit
of a complicated time for many countries,

00:07:38.579 --> 00:07:39.706
at the national level,

00:07:39.747 --> 00:07:41.582
around climate-change policy.

00:07:41.582 --> 00:07:43.334
But the exciting thing about housing

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:46.004
is a lot of the changes
that we need are superlocal ...

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:50.800
where small numbers of voters
can actually make a huge difference.

00:07:50.842 --> 00:07:52.010
So let's do this.

00:07:52.051 --> 00:07:55.555
Let's put climate and, importantly,
actions that drive climate finance,

00:07:55.596 --> 00:07:58.224
at the top of the agenda
for every local government.

00:07:58.266 --> 00:07:59.892
Let's write to our local officials

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:03.813
and say "Look, the banks and investors,
they're ready to put money to work.

00:08:03.855 --> 00:08:07.191
And if we can just put in place
the right policies, programs, permits,

00:08:07.191 --> 00:08:09.277
that money can come to our community."

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:12.613
Let's back the candidates
that back climate action

00:08:12.655 --> 00:08:14.866
and, importantly, let's show up to vote.

00:08:15.533 --> 00:08:17.452
OK, that's the first move --

00:08:17.452 --> 00:08:19.370
we can mobilize governments.

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:21.330
But if we want to mobilize big money,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.417
we also have to look
at the big balance sheets.

00:08:24.417 --> 00:08:26.169
So here's the second move.

00:08:26.544 --> 00:08:30.965
If you have an insurance policy
or a pension, this one's for you.

00:08:31.507 --> 00:08:34.302
You see, pension plans,
insurance companies,

00:08:34.302 --> 00:08:36.763
they're managing huge balance sheets.

00:08:37.388 --> 00:08:40.016
They're often referred to
as institutional investors.

00:08:40.016 --> 00:08:41.768
And these institutional investors,

00:08:41.768 --> 00:08:44.228
they're managing your money
for the long run.

00:08:44.854 --> 00:08:46.939
And the good news is that in recent years,

00:08:46.981 --> 00:08:48.983
they have increasingly begun to appreciate

00:08:48.983 --> 00:08:51.778
that the long-term risks associated
with climate change

00:08:51.819 --> 00:08:55.281
present a material risk to their ability
to manage your money.

00:08:55.281 --> 00:08:58.910
And so they've started taking action
to manage those climate risks.

00:08:58.951 --> 00:09:03.539
Well, thanks to the pandemic
and the recent focus on racial equity,

00:09:03.581 --> 00:09:05.792
they're now appreciating
that social inequities

00:09:05.833 --> 00:09:08.795
also create these long-term
financial risks,

00:09:08.795 --> 00:09:11.089
and they're ready
to step up and take action.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.340
Here's some good news --

00:09:12.381 --> 00:09:17.720
in 2020, investors financed over 140
billion dollars' worth of social bonds.

00:09:18.304 --> 00:09:21.474
That's up from 17 billion
just the year before.

00:09:21.516 --> 00:09:22.683
What are social bonds?

00:09:22.683 --> 00:09:27.188
They’re bonds [where] proceeds can be used
towards addressing social inequities,

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:29.774
including things like affordable housing.

00:09:29.774 --> 00:09:31.400
So when I talk to these investors

00:09:31.442 --> 00:09:34.112
and ask them, "OK, you're doing
billions, that's great,

00:09:34.112 --> 00:09:36.197
but what would it take
to get to trillions?"

00:09:36.197 --> 00:09:38.533
they say it all comes down
to having a mandate,

00:09:38.533 --> 00:09:40.076
a mandate to better integrate

00:09:40.118 --> 00:09:42.245
these environmental
or social considerations

00:09:42.286 --> 00:09:43.830
into how they manage your money,

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.165
the mandate to actually put
the money to work.

00:09:46.165 --> 00:09:48.543
So how can we give them that mandate?

00:09:48.543 --> 00:09:50.128
Well, there's two ways.

00:09:50.128 --> 00:09:52.213
One is insurance companies
and pension plans

00:09:52.255 --> 00:09:54.423
are increasingly giving you options

00:09:54.465 --> 00:09:57.927
to move your money from a general pool
into a more dedicated one

00:09:57.927 --> 00:10:00.054
that manages money
with greater environmental

00:10:00.096 --> 00:10:01.889
and social considerations in mind.

00:10:02.765 --> 00:10:05.268
And if you don't have
that option, demand it.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.812
Large pension plans, insurance companies,

00:10:07.812 --> 00:10:10.606
they have teams whose job it is
to listen to your concerns.

00:10:10.648 --> 00:10:13.526
I know, because that was one
of my first jobs out of college,

00:10:13.568 --> 00:10:16.320
to just answer phones
for a large pension plan in Canada.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:19.907
And the more we speak up
and influence these large investors

00:10:19.907 --> 00:10:21.242
who are managing our money

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:23.286
and make clear
how we want them to consider

00:10:23.286 --> 00:10:24.829
environmental and social causes,

00:10:24.871 --> 00:10:28.791
the more of a mandate they will have
to put those trillions to work.

00:10:28.791 --> 00:10:31.169
OK, so first move is governments,

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:33.212
second is the institutional investors.

00:10:33.838 --> 00:10:35.715
The third move is ourselves.

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:39.177
We need to create the demand
for these greener solutions.

00:10:39.218 --> 00:10:41.304
Let me give you a few examples.

00:10:41.345 --> 00:10:45.057
You know, most banks,
they'll offer a discount for your mortgage

00:10:45.057 --> 00:10:47.727
if it meets minimum
energy-efficiency standards.

00:10:48.102 --> 00:10:49.437
But guess what?

00:10:49.478 --> 00:10:51.898
Few people take them up on that offer.

00:10:51.898 --> 00:10:55.526
Similarly, I worked for a large
real-estate investment company.

00:10:55.568 --> 00:10:58.863
They were willing to do the right thing,
to retrofit their buildings

00:10:58.905 --> 00:11:00.239
and build entirely new ones

00:11:00.239 --> 00:11:04.118
with new materials that would make it
a low-carbon building altogether.

00:11:04.118 --> 00:11:05.703
But you know what they told me?

00:11:05.745 --> 00:11:07.788
Aside from a few markets in Europe,

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:09.749
they just weren't seeing the demand.

00:11:10.416 --> 00:11:14.003
So I think we can take some inspiration
from the other aspects of our lives,

00:11:14.003 --> 00:11:16.797
like the food we eat
or the vehicles we drive,

00:11:16.839 --> 00:11:18.841
where we have shown banks and investors

00:11:18.841 --> 00:11:21.427
that it's worth financing
alternatives to meat,

00:11:21.427 --> 00:11:23.137
or electric vehicles.

00:11:23.179 --> 00:11:26.265
Let's bring that shift now
into the homes in which we live,

00:11:26.307 --> 00:11:29.518
and let's show them that it's worth
financing more energy-efficient,

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:30.895
lower-carbon homes.

00:11:30.937 --> 00:11:33.272
So when you're out to buy
or rent your next home,

00:11:33.314 --> 00:11:34.941
ask about energy efficiency,

00:11:34.941 --> 00:11:36.108
and even better,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.069
ask about the overall carbon footprint.

00:11:39.403 --> 00:11:41.197
So there are three things we can do.

00:11:41.197 --> 00:11:42.615
We can activate governments.

00:11:42.615 --> 00:11:45.159
We can mobilize these large,
institutional investors.

00:11:45.201 --> 00:11:47.036
We can create the demand ourselves.

00:11:48.204 --> 00:11:51.999
You know, as anyone in finance
would hate to admit it,

00:11:51.999 --> 00:11:54.669
money doesn't make the world go around.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:12:00.424
This big, beautiful blue and green
planet that we live on,

00:12:00.424 --> 00:12:04.262
it's been spinning on its axis
for long before we were here

00:12:04.303 --> 00:12:07.014
and will continue to do so
for long after we've gone.

00:12:08.516 --> 00:12:10.685
What money can do is influence

00:12:10.685 --> 00:12:13.187
just how long we'll be here
to enjoy the ride.

00:12:14.063 --> 00:12:15.690
Thank you very much.

00:12:15.731 --> 00:12:17.191
(Cheers and applause)

